In accordance with O.C.G.A. § 48-13-112(c), such excise tax shall be:
(1) Imposed only at the time sales and use tax on the sale or use of such energy would have been due and payable under O.C.G.A. § 48-8-30 but for the exemption under O.C.G.A. § 48-8-3.2;
(2) Due and payable in the same manner as would be required under O.C.G.A. § 48-8-1 et seq., except as other provided in O.C.G.A. § 48-8-110 et seq.;
(3) A debt of the purchaser of energy until it is paid and shall be recoverable at law in the same manner as authorized for the recovery of other debts;
(4) Remitted to the governing authority of Peach County by the dealer collecting such excise tax; and
(5) A liability of the dealer at the applicable rate on the charges actually collected or the amount of excise taxes collected from purchasers, whichever is greater.
(Ord. of 9-10-2013, § 4)

751:
(a) As used in this section, the following definitions apply:
(1) Vehicle means every automobile, trailer, motor home, mobile home, boat, motorcycle or other vehicle which is self-propelled.
(2) Right-of-way means a public easement possessed by the county granting the right or privilege to the county for use by the general public for transportation, vehicular or otherwise, which generally includes county roadways and highways and extends to include sidewalks and park strips, if any.
(3) Trailer means a vehicle with or without motive power designed to be drawn by a motor vehicle; provided, however, that such term shall not include a mobile home as such term is set forth in Code section 8-2-160 unless such vehicle was left unattended on public property and its removal was requested by a peace officer.
(4) Public highway or public roadway carries the same definition as found in this chapter.
(b) Right-of-way, public highway, and public roadway parking prohibited. Except where otherwise allowed by law, no person shall stop, leave standing or park any vehicle or trailer, whether attended or unattended, on any portion of a right-of-way, public highway, or public roadway, including sidewalks or other pedestrian access routes adjacent to the right-of-way, within the county highways, county road system, or on extensions of a county road system, except when necessary to avoid conflict with other traffic, emergency circumstances, mechanical breakdown, or in compliance with the directions of a peace offer or an official traffic control device.
(c) Parking prohibited in specified places. Except when necessary to avoid conflict with other traffic, emergency circumstances, mechanical breakdown, or in compliance with law or the directions of a peace officer or official traffic-control device, no person shall park any vehicle or trailer:
(1) On the roadway side of any vehicle stopped or parked in an appropriately designated area at the edge of a curb of a street;
(2) Within an intersection;
(3) Within or on a crosswalk;
(4) Alongside or opposite any street excavation, roadwork, or other obstruction when stopping, standing, or parking would obstruct traffic;
(5) Upon any bridge, elevated structure, or tunnel;
(6) On any railroad tracks;
(7) On any controlled-access highway;
(8) In the area between roadways of a divided highway, including crossovers;
(9) In front of a public or private driveway;
(10) Within 15 feet of a fire hydrant;
(11) Within 20 feet of a crosswalk at an intersection;
(12) Within 30 feet upon the approach to any flashing signal, stop sign, yield sign, or traffic-control signal located at the side of a roadway; or
(13) At any place where official signs prohibit parking.
(d) Parking for certain purposes prohibited. Unless otherwise permitted by law or by the governing authority, no person shall park a vehicle or trailer upon a right-of-way, public highway, public roadway, or designated public parking area for:
(1) Displaying the vehicle or trailer for sale;
(2) Washing, greasing or repairing the vehicle or trailer, except repairs necessitated by sudden emergency, and, in the case of mechanical breakdown, the vehicle shall be moved with all due haste; or
(3) Displaying or advertising.
(e) Overnight stay prohibited. No person shall park on a county street, roadway, or right-of-way for the purpose of leaving the vehicle overnight, whether attended or unattended, absent emergency circumstances.
(f) Impounding. Any vehicle or trailer that is parked in violation of this chapter for 24 consecutive hours may be impounded by the county, or its designee, in the same manner as for other parking violations. Any person claiming an impounded vehicle or trailer shall produce evidence of their identity and ownership. The person claiming the vehicle or trailer shall pay the storage costs and towing charges to regain possession of the vehicle. The county, or its designee, may remove the vehicle or trailer immediately if the vehicle or trailer constitutes a safety or traffic hazard.
(g) Violation. Any person who violates this section shall be punishable by the following fines:
(1) First offense, a minimum fine of $100.00.
(2) Second offense within a one-year period of the first offense, a minimum fine of $150.00.
(3) Third offense within a one-year period of the first offense, a minimum fine of $200.00.
(4) Fourth offense within a one-year period of the first offense, a minimum fine of $300.00, with a mandatory appearance in magistrate court.
(Ord. of 11-14-2023(2), Exh. B)
